<%# The Standard page template (`page_templates` in config/theme/default.yml):
    the page's heading, its blocks and its subpages. What a template is handed
    is three locals, `page`, `post` and `list_block` (`post` and `list_block`
    nil but on a post's own detail view), and it reads them and the contract's
    helpers, no instance variable. %>
<%# The page's own heading -- skipped at a post's own detail view, since
    Posts::DetailComponent (which replaced the old content_blocks partial
    _post_list_detail) already renders that post's title as the response's
    one <h1>. %>
<% if post.nil? %>
  <header class="mb-12">
    <% tagline = translated(theme_site, :tagline) if page.root? %>
    <% if tagline.present? %>
      <p class="font-cs-mono text-label uppercase tracking-mono text-meta"><%= tagline %></p>
    <% end %>
    <h1 class="mt-3 font-semibold leading-tight tracking-tight <%= page.root? ? "text-display" : "text-h1" %>"><%= translated(page, :title) %></h1>
  </header>
<% end %>
<%# A page's body is its blocks in `position` order (the association is
    ordered), each rendered by its own registered type's ViewComponent
    (`Theme.current.component_for`, open-types spec §5) rather than a partial
    named after `block_type` -- the registry, not the view path, is what a
    plugin's block type or an alternate theme now hooks into.

    The one exception is a post detail request (`/en/blog/my-post`): the block
    the controller matched the post under renders that single post, through
    its content type's `detail_component`, in place of its listing, while the
    page's other blocks -- its intro text, its image -- still render around
    it. `list_block` is the block the controller resolved the post under,
    and `render_block` compares against it rather than asking
    `block.post_list?`, so that a page carrying more than one listing swaps
    out only that block.

    A stored `block_type` that isn't (or is no longer) registered --
    `block.definition` is nil -- renders nothing rather than 500ing the public
    site (global constraints): a removed plugin's type must degrade
    gracefully for whoever already has it on a page, not take the whole
    request down.

    The dispatch itself is `ThemeHelper#render_block`. Only the top-level
    blocks are walked here: a section renders the blocks in it through the
    same helper (`Blocks::SectionComponent`), so each block is drawn once.
    The outline is the page's one (`ThemeHelper#block_outline`), which the
    sections ask too. %>
<% block_outline(page).top_level.each do |block| %><%= render_block(block) %><% end %>
<%# The tree is otherwise reachable only by typing an exact URL -- this is what
    makes clicking from a page down into its children possible at all.
    Published only, matching every other public listing on this site, and
    kept only: a page in the trash keeps its `published_at` but has no URL
    any more (`PublicSite::Paths#compute_path`), so "published" alone would
    list it by name with a link to nowhere. Hiding trashed records is
    explicit at every such query (`Trashable` has no default scope); this
    one is pinned in `test/integration/trash_visibility_test.rb`. %>
<%# Their titles are the published ones. %>
<%# The list is navigation, named by its mono label (`aria-labelledby`, so a
    screen reader's landmarks say "In this section" and not a second unnamed
    "navigation"), and its links are set as the header's menu is: dim, ink on
    hover, no underline, one per line. A post's own page draws none (O7): its
    listing page's subpages are that page's, and the post is not one of them.
    Nothing without subpages -- no label, no rule -- so the label is inside the
    same `if` as the rule. %>
<% children = post.nil? ? published_views(page.children.kept.published) : [] # public: rows (the subpages' membership and order are the tree's; titles come from published_views) %>
<% if children.any? %>
  <nav class="page-children mt-12 border-t border-line pt-6" aria-labelledby="page-children-label">
    <p id="page-children-label" class="font-cs-mono text-label uppercase tracking-mono text-meta"><%= site_string(:in_this_section) %></p>
    <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2">
      <% children.each do |child| %>
        <li><%= link_to translated(child, :title), page_url_path(child), class: "text-dim hover:text-ink" %></li>
      <% end %>
    </ul>
  </nav>
<% end %>
